# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# handOffs/delta-log.md — union merge (S354)
#
# The log is APPEND-ONLY and its entries are one-line records. Every collision
# observed has been a tail-append conflict: two concurrent sessions each append
# after the same last entry. `union` keeps BOTH sides automatically instead of
# raising a conflict that has to be hand-resolved — which is where the real
# damage came from, since a hand-resolve once renumbered the OTHER session's
# already-merged entries.
#
# ⚑ This trades a merge conflict for a possible DUPLICATE sequence number, and
# that trade is only safe because `bun scripts/delta-lint.ts` is loud about
# duplicates in CI. A duplicate is not cosmetic: the flogence bridge uses the
# sequence as a checkpoint cursor, so the second entry to merge reads as
# already-absorbed and drops out of the digest. The two changes land together;
# neither is sufficient alone.
handOffs/delta-log.md merge=union

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hand-off.md — pin the stored line endings to LF (S436)
#
# PR #1068 committed this file as CRLF from a Windows clone (raw blob: 4141 CR
# bytes against 4139 lines). `git diff` then reported ONE hunk covering the whole
# file with no common context, so that landing had no reviewable diff and `git
# blame` across it is useless; #1065 flipped it back to LF the next day, producing
# a second whole-file hunk. Content survived both — verified by normalising the
# blobs and re-diffing — but the review surface did not, and two lone CRs inside
# lines corrupted four worktree paths in the S432 pickup (repaired at S436).
#
# This file is the one every session reads and appends to, from clones with
# differing `core.autocrlf` settings, so it is the one that must not depend on
# the committer's local setting.
hand-off.md text eol=lf

# scripts/ shell tooling + the git hooks — pin stored AND checked-out line endings to LF (S453).
# A CRLF checkout breaks a shebang on Linux ("bad interpreter: No such file or directory"), and
# `core.autocrlf=true` on the Windows clones makes that the DEFAULT checkout form. The hooks are
# extensionless, so they are listed explicitly. Surfaced while committing merge-on-green.sh, whose
# own working copy git warned it would convert on the next touch; the exposure is pre-existing and
# repo-wide for these files, not introduced by that script.
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/git-hooks/* text eol=lf
